hexagon: parse the environment of the backend init without a crash or a hang
Fold-into: upstream/hexagon-host

Base: llama.cpp commit c6824a9 plus every patch of the series before this one.

ggml_hexagon_init reads its switches from the environment. Six values gave a
crash, an exception out of the init, undefined behavior or a hang:

- An empty GGML_HEXAGON_PROFILE list: vec_to_str called pop_back on an empty
  string.
- A GGML_HEXAGON_OPFILTER value that is not a valid regex: the constructor of
  std::regex threw out of the init.
- A GGML_HEXAGON_PROFILE item that is not a number or is too large: std::stoul
  threw out of the init.
- A large GGML_HEXAGON_OPBATCH: the default trace size opbatch * 256
  overflowed int.
- A physical range "A-B" of GGML_HEXAGON_DEVICES with a large B: the loop over
  the range does a linear search in each step, thus a large range takes a very
  long time, and B = INT_MAX never stops.
- A device group of more than 16 physical devices, from ranges and single
  indices together: the group has arrays of 16 entries
  (ggml_hexagon_mdev_group::fence_slots).

This patch gives an empty string for an empty list. For a regex that is not
valid it logs a warning and uses no op filter. For a profile list that does
not parse it logs a warning and turns profiling off. It computes the trace
size in 64 bits and clamps it to the int range. It ignores, with a warning, a
range that starts below 0, ends before its start, or holds more than 16
devices, and it cuts a device group to 16 physical devices with a warning.
The app sets only GGML_HEXAGON_OPFUSION and GGML_HEXAGON_OPFUSION_STATE, thus
its behavior does not change.

